{"records":[{"id":"nz5mch8k2w","title":"Multifaceted N-degron recognition and ubiquitylation by GID/CTLH E3 ligases","description":"N-degron E3 ubiquitin ligases recognize specific residues at the N-termini of substrates.  Although molecular details of N-degron recognition are known for several E3 ligases, the range of N-terminal motifs that can bind a given E3 substrate binding domain remains unclear.  Here, we discovered capacity of Gid4 and Gid10 substrate receptor subunits of yeast “GID”/human “CTLH” multiprotein E3 ligases to tightly bind a wide range of N-terminal residues whose recognition is determined in part by the downstream sequence context.  Screening of phage displaying peptide libraries with exposed N-termini identified novel consensus motifs with non-Pro N-terminal residues binding Gid4 or Gid10 with high affinity.  Structural data reveal that conformations of flexible loops in Gid4 and Gid10 complement sequences and folds of interacting peptides.  Together with analysis of endogenous substrate degrons, the data show that degron identity, substrate domains harboring targeted lysines, and varying E3 ligase higher-order assemblies combinatorially determine efficiency of ubiquitylation and degradation.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-16T20:56:17.845Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nz5mch8k2w","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nz5mch8k2w"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Brenda","last_name":"Schulman","orcid":"0000-0002-3083-1126"},{"first_name":"Jakub","last_name":"Chrustowicz","orcid":"0000-0002-3059-1863"},{"first_name":"Dawafuti ","last_name":"Sherpa","orcid":"0000-0001-7815-5895"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rfpsg6939c","title":"GID E3 ligase supramolecular chelate assembly configures multipronged ubiquitin targeting of an oligomeric metabolic enzyme. Sherpa et al.","description":"How are E3 ubiquitin ligases configured to match substrate quaternary structures?  Here, by studying the yeast GID complex, mutation of which is Glucose-Induced Degradation deficient, we discover supramolecular chelate assembly as an E3 ligase strategy for targeting an oligomeric substrate. Cryo EM structures show that to bind the tetrameric substrate fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase (Fbp1), two otherwise functional GID E3s assemble into a 20-protein Chelator-GIDSR4, which resembles an organometallic supramolecular chelate. The Chelator-GIDSR4 assembly avidly binds multiple Fbp1 degrons and positions Fbp1 so that its protomers are simultaneously ubiquitylated at lysines near its allosteric and substrate binding sites. Significantly, key structural and biochemical features - including capacity for supramolecular assembly - are preserved in the human ortholog, the CTLH E3. Based on our integrative structural, biochemical and cell biological data, we propose that higher-order E3 ligase assembly generally underlies multipronged targeting, capable of simultaneously incapacitating multiple protomers and functionalities of oligomeric substrates.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-04-26T15:23:09.81Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rfpsg6939c","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rfpsg6939c"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Brenda","last_name":"Schulman","orcid":"0000-0002-3083-1126"},{"first_name":"Dawafuti ","last_name":"Sherpa","orcid":"0000-0001-7815-5895"},{"first_name":"Jakub","last_name":"Chrustowicz","orcid":"0000-0002-3059-1863"},{"first_name":"Christine ","last_name":"Langlois","orcid":"0000-0003-1567-3577"},{"first_name":"Laura-Anna","last_name":"Hehl","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Arno","last_name":"Alpi","orcid":"0000-0002-9572-7266"},{"first_name":"Karthik Varma","last_name":"Gottemukkala","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Cell Biology","Ubiquitylation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vv8spnwgyr","title":"Activity-based proteomic dataset for EGF stimulated HEK293T cells","description":"These raw data are from proteomic experiments where HEK293T cells have been treated with or without epidermal growth factor. Using a photocrosslinking activity-based probe for ubiquitin E3 ligases, the treated and untreated cells are profiled. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-12-04T12:47:05.071Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vv8spnwgyr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vv8spnwgyr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Satpal","last_name":"Virdee","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Structural Proteomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"32h94v2yv2","title":"Elucidation of E3 Ubiquitin Ligase Specificity . Zhang et al.Through Proteome-wide Degron Mapping. Zhang et al.","description":"This repository contains sequencing data related to \"Elucidation of E3 Ubiquitin Ligase Specificity Through Proteome-wide Degron Mapping\" by Zhang et al. Data is included for the GPS peptidome screen with and without MLN, results from the composition-based SVM machine learning model, along with sequencing data for scanning and saturation mutagenesis degron footprints, and from CRISPR screens for identification of E3-degron pairs. Westernblot raw images from cycloheximide chase and co-immunoprecipitation assays are also included.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-09-21T06:47:33.015Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/32h94v2yv2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/32h94v2yv2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhiqian","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Brandon","last_name":"Sie","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Aiquan","last_name":"Chang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yumei","last_name":"Leng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christopher","last_name":"Nardone","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Stephen ","last_name":"Elledge","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Protein Degradation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xxxwsrsydr","title":"What Makes an Effective PROTAC? Unlocking Drug-Resistant Pathways Through Warhead, Linker, and Anchor Modifications","description":"Research Paper Description;\nThis research investigates the structural design of PROTACs (Proteolysis-Targeting Chimeras), a novel class of therapeutic molecules that induce targeted protein degradation by recruiting specific E3 ubiquitin ligases to disease-related proteins. The study explores how variations in the three key components of PROTACs , the warhead (target-binding ligand), the linker (spacer), and the anchor (E3 ligase ligand) , influence their ability to reach and degrade proteins that are typically inaccessible to traditional small molecule drugs. By analyzing five theoretically designed PROTAC candidates targeting CDK9, a kinase implicated in cancer, this paper aims to highlight design strategies that optimize selectivity, binding cooperativity, and degradation efficiency. The work also emphasizes the potential of PROTACs to act as “smart drugs,” capable of bypassing limitations in conventional drug therapy by hijacking the cell’s own protein disposal system.\n\nResearch Paper done by an 11th grader from Bali, Indonesia from online school: Wolsey Hall Oxford.\n\nLicense:\nThis work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 4.0 International License.\nYou may share and adapt it with credit, non-commercially, and under the same license. Credit must be given to the original author: Ell Siti Macpherson.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-07-15T18:13:57.716Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xxxwsrsydr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xxxwsrsydr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ell","last_name":"Siti Macpherson","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Chemistry","Healthcare Research"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"bwdj9tvssg","title":"RNF168 E3 ligase participates in ubiquitin signaling and recruitment of SLX4 during DNA crosslink repair. Katsuki et al","description":"SLX4/FANCP is a key Fanconi anemia (FA) protein and a DNA repair scaffold for incision around a DNA interstrand crosslink (ICL) by its partner XPF nuclease. The tandem UBZ4 ubiquitin-binding domains of SLX4 are critical for the recruitment of SLX4 to damage sites, likely via binding to K63-linked polyubiquitin chains. However, the identity of the ubiquitin E3 ligase that mediates SLX4 recruitment remains unknown. Using siRNA screening with a GFP-tagged N-terminal half of SLX4 (termed SLX4-N), we identified RNF168 E3 ligase as a critical factor for mitomycin C (MMC)-induced SLX4 foci formation. RNF168 and GFP-SLX4-N colocalized in MMC-induced ubiquitin foci. Accumulation of SLX4-N at psoralen-laser ICL tracks or of endogenous SLX4 at Digoxigenin-psoralen/UVA ICL was dependent on RNF168. Finally, we found that RNF168 was epistatic with SLX4 in promoting MMC tolerance. We conclude that RNF168 is a critical component of the signal transduction that recruits SLX4 to ICL damage.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-10-13T14:24:13.979Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bwdj9tvssg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bwdj9tvssg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yoko","last_name":"Katsuki","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Minoru","last_name":"Takata","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Cell Biology","DNA Repair","Fanconi Anemia","Ubiquitylation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"sxpfkty7dw","title":"Raw data related to DELTEX2 C-terminal domain recognizes and recruits ADP-ribosylated proteins for ubiquitination","description":"Crosstalk between ubiquitination and ADP-ribosylation regulates spatio-temporal recruitment of key players during DNA damage repair (DDR). The Deltex family of ubiquitin ligases (DTX1–4 and DTX3L), are characterized by a RING domain followed by a C-terminal domain (DTC) of unknown function; four Deltex proteins have other domains or partner proteins for binding poly-ADP-ribose (PAR), suggesting a role for these proteins in mediating crosstalk between ubiquitination and ADP-ribosylation. Here, we use two label-free mass spectrometry techniques to identify substrates of human DTX2 and uncover a new ADP-ribose-binding domain that facilitates PAR-dependent ubiquitination.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-09-10T18:20:54.757Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/sxpfkty7dw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/sxpfkty7dw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Syed Feroj","last_name":"Ahmed","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lori","last_name":"Buetow","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Danny","last_name":"Huang ","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ggy5rrpgjs","title":"Biochemical properties of PD-L1 ubiquitination by CRL3SPOP, ARIH1 and NEDD4 family ubiquitin ligases","description":"As a key immune checkpoint ligand, PD-L1 represents a critical target in cancer immunotherapy. While multiple E3 ubiquitin ligases including CRL3SPOP, ARIH1, and NEDD4 have been implicated in PD-L1 degradation, their precise enzymatic mechanisms remain unclear.  Through in vitro reconstitution with purified components, we systematically compared the enzymatic activities of CRL3SPOP, ARIH1, and NEDD4 ligases on PD-L1's cytoplasmic domain. ARIH1, rather than CRL3SPOP, could independently ubiquitinate PD-L1. We demonstrated a unique mechanism that ARIH1 acts as a substrate receptor to cooperate with CRLs to catalyze PD-L1 ubiquitination. We also biochemically validated the E3 ligase activity of the NEDD4 family E3s towards PD-L1. Using liposomes in the enzymatic assays, we illustrated that the phosphorylation enhanced PD-L1 ubiquitination by disrupting its membrane association. Our study provides previously neglected biochemical insights into PD-L1 ubiquitination, which advances our understanding of the molecular details of PD-L1 regulation mechanism. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-04-25T09:53:17.75Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ggy5rrpgjs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ggy5rrpgjs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"guojiao","last_name":"xie","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer Immunology","Programmed Death-Ligand 1","Ubiquitin Ligase"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"694w27wyjm","title":"Raw data related to Locking CBL TKBD in its native conformation presents a novel therapeutic opportunity in mutant CBL-dependent leukemia","description":"Casitas B-lineage lymphoma (CBL) negatively regulates receptor protein tyrosine kinases (RTKs). Deleterious CBL mutants lose their E3 ubiquitin ligase activity and gain function as adaptors to cause myeloproliferative diseases. Currently, no targeted treatment is available for patients with CBL mutant-dependent diseases. We discovered a nanomolar-affinity peptide inhibitor, CBLock, by using a combination of phage-display technology and structure-based optimization. CBLock binds the substrate-binding pocket in CBL’s N-terminal tyrosine kinase binding domain (TKBD). The interaction between CBL mutants and RTKs could be disrupted by CBLock, thereby impairing the adaptor function of CBL mutants and downstream RTK signaling pathways. Therefore, inhibiting CBL TKBD in its native state presents a promising therapeutic opportunity in targeting mutant CBL-dependent blood cancers. Here, we present the western blot and SDS-PAGE raw data related to this work.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-04-28T07:38:46.492Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/694w27wyjm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/694w27wyjm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Syed Feroj","last_name":"Ahmed","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Danny","last_name":"Huang","orcid":"0000-0002-6192-259X"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot","Sodium Dodecyl Sulfate Polyacrylamide Gel Electrophoresis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"tbwzsgzwcy","title":"CTDP1 and RPB7 Stabilize Pol II and Permit Reinitiation","description":"The mechanisms governing the termination and subsequent reinitiation of RNA polymerase II (Pol II) remain poorly understood. Here we find that depletion of RPB7 leads to the destabilization of Pol II’s largest subunit, RPB1. This destabilization is influenced by the loop regions of RPB7, CDK9, the C-terminal domain (CTD) of RPB1, and its linker region. The stabilization process of RPB1 is regulated by the E3 ubiquitin ligase Cullin 3. Additionally, RPB7 interacts with the phosphatase CTDP1, which is crucial for maintaining RPB1 stability. RPB7 is also vital for the reinitiation of Pol II, engages with RNA processing factors, and is localized to the RNA exit channel of the Pol II complex. The absence of RPB7 compromises RNA processing. We propose that RPB7 recruits CTDP1 to dephosphorylate Pol II, enhancing its stability and facilitating efficient reinitiation, adding an emerging dimension to transcriptional regulation.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-02-07T23:31:41.583Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tbwzsgzwcy","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tbwzsgzwcy"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"dexun","last_name":"ji","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Transcription","Protein Degradation","Phosphatase","RNA Polymerase"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":122747,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":150,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":122747,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":122430,"restricted":60,"embargoed":257},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2188},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":610},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":463},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":417},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":404},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":389},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":361},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":314},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":273},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":249}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":120145},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1899},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":182},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":127},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":68},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":56},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":41},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":30},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":622},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":471},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":398},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":359},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":310}]}}